---
import { buildRule110Selectors } from '../../demos/rule-110/build-rule-110-selectors';
import { rule110CellId } from '../../demos/rule-110/rule-110-cell-id';
import type { Rule110Selectors } from '../../demos/rule-110/rule-110-selectors';
import DemoLayout from '../../layouts/DemoLayout.astro';

const width: number = 16;
const height: number = 12;
const rows: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: height }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index);
const columns: ReadonlyArray<number> = Array.from({ length: width }, (_: unknown, index: number): number => index);

// Rule 110 itself, as CSS: written at build time by src/demos/rule-110, for this size of grid.
const selectors: Rule110Selectors = buildRule110Selectors(width, height);
function prefixed(list: ReadonlyArray<string>): string {
    return list.map((selector: string): string => `.rule-110 ${selector}`).join(',\n');
}
const ruleCss: string = [
    `${prefixed(selectors.on)} { --state: var(--cell-on); }`,
    `${prefixed(selectors.missing)} { --state: var(--cell-missing); }`,
    `${prefixed(selectors.wrong)} { --state: var(--cell-wrong); }`,
    `${prefixed(selectors.focus)} { outline: 2px solid var(--focus); outline-offset: 1px; }`,
].join('\n');
const ruleCount: number = selectors.on.length + selectors.missing.length + selectors.wrong.length;

const cellExample: string = `/* Should be on, because the cell above is on and the one above-right is off */
#r0c4:checked ~ #r0c5:not(:checked) ~ #r1c4:not(:checked) ~ [for="r1c4"] {
    /* draw it as "click me" */
}`;
---
<DemoLayout path="/Demos/Rule-110">
    <div slot="demo" class="rule-110-stage">
        <div class="rule-110">
            {rows.map((row: number) => columns.map((column: number) => (
                <input class="cell-box" type="checkbox" id={rule110CellId(row, column)} checked={row === 0 && column === width - 1} />
            )))}
            {rows.map((row: number) => columns.map((column: number) => (
                <label class:list={['cell', { 'is-start': row === 0 }]} for={rule110CellId(row, column)}>
                    <span class="visually-hidden">Row {row + 1}, cell {column + 1}</span>
                </label>
            )))}
        </div>
        <ul class="key" role="list">
            <li><span class="swatch swatch-on"></span>On</li>
            <li><span class="swatch swatch-missing"></span>Should be on: click it</li>
            <li><span class="swatch swatch-wrong"></span>Should be off: click it</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
    <Fragment set:html={`<style>${ruleCss}</style>`} />

    <p>
        The top row is the starting pattern: click its cells to change it. Every row below follows from the row above, by
        Rule 110. CSS works out which cells should be on and marks them; your job is to click them, one row at a time.
        Get one wrong and CSS shows it in red.
    </p>

    <h2>The rule</h2>
    <p>
        Each cell looks at the three cells above it: above-left, directly above, and above-right. There are eight possible
        patterns, and Rule 110 says which of them switch the cell on:
    </p>
    <div class="table-responsive">
        <table class="rule-table">
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Above</th>
                    <td>111</td><td>110</td><td>101</td><td>100</td><td>011</td><td>010</td><td>001</td><td>000</td>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="row">Cell</th>
                    <td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p>
        Read the bottom row as a binary number, 01101110, and you get 110: that's where the name comes from. It looks
        like a toy, but in 2004 Matthew Cook published a proof that Rule 110, given a wide enough row and long enough, can
        compute anything a computer can. It's Turing-complete.
    </p>

    <h2>How it works</h2>
    <p>
        Every cell is a checkbox, so HTML holds the whole grid. The boxes all come first in the page, in reading order,
        with the labels you see drawn after them. That order matters: CSS's sibling combinator, <code>~</code>, can only
        look forward, so a cell's label can be reached from the three boxes above it and from its own box:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{cellExample}</code></pre>
    <p>
        Rule 110 needs three conditions to say when a cell should be on, and two to say when it should be off. Repeat
        those for every cell in a grid of {width} by {height} and you get {ruleCount} selectors. Nobody wrote them by
        hand: a small program wrote them when the site was built, and tests check that its conditions match the rule table
        above exactly. That's no different from a compiler turning code into machine instructions; but it's worth saying,
        because it's part of the honest answer.
    </p>

    <h2>You are the clock</h2>
    <p>
        CSS knows what every row should be, but it can't make it so. A rule can describe a state; it can't tick a box. So
        the machine only moves forward when you click, and it stops when the grid runs out. That's why this demo is
        both the strongest evidence that HTML and CSS can compute anything, and the clearest picture of what they can't
        do: run on their own.
    </p>
</DemoLayout>

<style>
    .rule-110-stage {
        display: grid;
        gap: 1.25rem;
        justify-items: center;
    }

    .rule-110 {
        --cell-on: var(--heading);
        --cell-missing: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-amber) 55%, transparent);
        --cell-wrong: var(--danger);

        display: grid;
        grid-template-columns: repeat(16, minmax(0, 2rem));
        gap: 3px;
        width: 100%;
        max-width: 36rem;
    }

    /* The boxes stay in the page, so the grid can be used with a keyboard, but out of sight behind their labels. */
    .cell-box {
        position: absolute;
        width: 1px;
        height: 1px;
        opacity: 0;
    }

    .cell {
        --state: var(--surface-alt);
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        border-radius: 3px;
        background: var(--state);
        cursor: pointer;
        corner-shape: round;
    }

    .cell.is-start {
        box-shadow: inset 0 0 0 1px var(--border-strong);
    }

    .cell:hover {
        outline: 2px solid var(--border-strong);
    }

    .key {
        display: flex;
        flex-wrap: wrap;
        justify-content: center;
        gap: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
        margin: 0;
        padding: 0;
        color: var(--text-muted);
        font-size: var(--text-sm);
    }

    .key li {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        gap: 0.5rem;
        margin: 0;
    }

    .swatch {
        width: 1rem;
        aspect-ratio: 1;
        border-radius: 3px;
        corner-shape: round;
    }

    .swatch-on { background: var(--heading); }
    .swatch-missing { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--brand-amber) 55%, transparent); }
    .swatch-wrong { background: var(--danger); }

    .rule-table td,
    .rule-table th {
        font-family: var(--font-mono);
        text-align: center;
    }
</style>
